fuckwayland for Debian/Ubuntu
=============================

One package, both releases
--------------------------

The package is Architecture: all and every module it ships is pure Python
standard library, installed into the version-independent
/usr/lib/python3/dist-packages.  The same .deb therefore installs on Ubuntu
24.04 (Python 3.12) and 26.04 (Python 3.14); nothing in it is compiled against
one of them.  The .pyc files are made on your machine, by your python3, when
the package is configured.

What is installed where
-----------------------

  /usr/bin/{wdotool,wwmctl,wxprop,wxrandr,warandr,wmirror}
        the six commands.  Under their own names only: no file of xdotool,
        wmctrl, x11-utils or x11-xserver-utils is touched or replaced, which
        is what keeps the X11 handover working.
  /usr/lib/python3/dist-packages/{wdotool,wwmctl,wxprop,wxrandr,warandr,wmirror}/
        the code.
  /usr/share/gnome-shell/extensions/fuckwayland-bridge@fuckwayland/
        the GNOME Shell bridge extension, system-wide.
  /etc/xdg/autostart/fuckwayland-enable-bridge.desktop
  /usr/lib/fuckwayland/enable-bridge
        the once-per-user helper that enables the extension (see below).
  /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-fuckwayland-uinput.rules
  /usr/lib/modules-load.d/fuckwayland-uinput.conf
        /dev/uinput for the user at the active seat.
  /usr/share/applications/warandr.desktop
        the menu entry for the GUI.

The GNOME extension, and why a re-login is the one manual step
--------------------------------------------------------------

gnome-shell scans extension directories only when a session starts, and
whether an extension is enabled is a per-user dconf setting.  dpkg runs as
root, possibly with no user session on the machine at all, so it can do
neither of those things itself.  What the package does instead:

  * installs the extension where gnome-shell will find it at the next login
    (the system directory -- no user's home is written to);
  * ships an autostart entry that runs /usr/lib/fuckwayland/enable-bridge
    inside each user's first GNOME session after the install.  That script
    adds the uuid to org.gnome.shell enabled-extensions once, writes a stamp
    in ~/.config/fuckwayland, and never touches the setting again -- so if you
    later disable the extension, it stays disabled.

So: install, log out, log back in, and the window commands work.  Before that
relogin wxrandr, warandr and wdotool's input commands already work, and the
window commands say the bridge is not running.

  gnome-extensions list --enabled | grep fuckwayland   # is it on for me?
  sh gnome/install-bridge.sh --check                   # the full report

If you had previously installed the extension per user with
gnome/install-bridge.sh (no --system), that copy lives in
~/.local/share/gnome-shell/extensions and shadows the packaged one.  Remove it
with `sh gnome/install-bridge.sh --uninstall` to use the package's copy.

/dev/uinput
-----------

The rule tags the node `uaccess`, so systemd-logind hands the user of the
active seat an ACL on it at every login; postinst applies it to the running
system as well, so no reboot is needed.  The node itself stays root:root 0600
-- no `input` group, no standing grant to anyone who is not at the seat.
Removing the package puts the node back to root:root 0600 with no ACL.

Anyone who can open /dev/uinput can type as you.  That is the trade these
tools exist to make, and it is spelled out in the threat model in
/usr/share/doc/fuckwayland/README.md.gz.  If it is the wrong trade on this
machine, remove the rule and run the input commands under sudo instead:

  sudo rm /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-fuckwayland-uinput.rules
  sudo udevadm control --reload-rules

Installing over the originals
-----------------------------

Deliberately not done by the package.  /usr/local/bin comes first on Ubuntu's
PATH, so it is one symlink per tool and no packaged file is disturbed:

  sudo ln -sfn /usr/bin/wdotool /usr/local/bin/xdotool
  sudo ln -sfn /usr/bin/wwmctl  /usr/local/bin/wmctrl
  sudo ln -sfn /usr/bin/wxprop  /usr/local/bin/xprop
  sudo ln -sfn /usr/bin/wxrandr /usr/local/bin/xrandr

